68†Terje Aasland (Labour Party) L89:     * cite69†Ministry of Finance cite70†Jens Stoltenberg (Labour Party) L90:     * cite71†Ministry of Foreign Affairs cite72†Espen Barth Eide (Labour Party) cite73†Åsmund Aukrust (Labour Party) L91:     * cite74†Ministry of Health and Care Services cite75†Jan Christian Vestre (Labour Party) L92:     * cite76†Ministry of Industry, Commerce and Fisheries cite77†Cecilie Myrseth (Labour Party) cite78†Marianne Sivertsen Næss (Labour Party) L93:     * cite79†Ministry of Justice and Public Security cite80†Astri Aas-Hansen (Labour Party) L94:     * cite81†Ministry of Labour and Social Inclusion cite82†Kjersti Stenseng (Labour Party) L95:     * cite83†Ministry of Local Government and Regional Development cite84†Bjørnar Selnes Skjæran (Labour Party) L96:     * cite85†Ministry of Transport cite86†Kamzy Gunaratnam (Labour Party) L97:   * cite87†The Government L98: \nL99: cite87†The Government L100: \nL101:     * cite88†Støre’s Government L102:       * cite88†Members of the Government L103:     * cite89†Find news L104:     * cite90†How is Norway governed? L105:       * cite91†Change of Government in Norway L106:       * cite92†How is Norway governed? L107:     * cite93†Norway's Governments since 1814 L108:       * cite94†Norway's Governments 1814 - L109:       * cite95†Statistics L110: \nL111: cite48†Historical archive L112: \nL113: cite96†State ownership of land in Svalbard L114: \nL115:   * [Button: Share ]\nL116: \nL117: Share\nL118:     * cite97†Facebook†www.facebook.com L119:     * cite98†X/Twitter†twitter.com L120:     * cite99†LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com L121:     * Epost\nL122: \nL123:   * [Button: Print]\nL124: # State ownership of land in Svalbard\nL125: \nL126: Date: 27/06/2024 | cite76†Ministry of Industry, Commerce and Fisheries L127: \nL128: The Norwegian state owns 98.75 per cent of all land in Svalbard. Through its ownership in Kings Bay AS and Bjørnøen AS, the state indirectly owns a further 0.75 per cent of land in the archipelago.\nL129: \nL130: cite100†Image: Adventdalen Svalbard L131: \nL132: Adventdalen Svalbard Credit: NFD\nL133: In addition to land, the state owns infrastructure and building stock as well as some infrastructure in Longyearbyen. The state owned land and state owned buildings on Svalbard is not for sale.\nL134: The Svalbard Policy\nL135: The Norwegian Ministry of Trade, Industry and Fisheries (the Ministry) owns and administers the state’s real property in Svalbard in accorcdance with the objectives of the Svalbard policy.\nL136: The overriding objectives of the Svalbard policy are:\nL137:   * Consistent and firm enforcement of sovereignty\nL138:   * Proper observance to the Svalbard Treaty and control to ensure compliance with the Treaty\nL139:   * Maintenance of peace and stability in the area\nL140:   * Preservation of the area’s distinctive natural wilderness\nL141:   * Maintenance of Norwegian communities in the archipelago\nL142: Through its ownership of the land in Longyearbyen, the state will, in consultation with the Longyearbyen Community Council as the planning authority, facilitate development within the scope of the objectives of the Svalbard policy.\nL143: Use of state owned land\nL144: Any queries regarding long and short term use of state owned land or buildings on Svalbard, may be directed to the Ministry’s Svalbard Office in Longyearbyen.\nL145: If you are selling or buying housing, cabins, appartements, boathouses, storage units etc. on Svalbard, you need the consent of the Norwegian government, i.e. the Norwegian Ministry of Trade, Industry and Fisheries, as the state owns the land on which the building sits.\nL146: The usage of state owned land is regulated through a ground rent agreement with the Ministry. You also need to ensure that the building itself is used in compliance with approvals from the planning authority and adheres to other regulations such as the Svalbard Environmental Protection Act (Svalbardmiljøloven) and the Svalbard Act (Svalbardloven).\nL147: cite76†Ministry of Industry, Commerce and Fisheries L148: ## Contact\nL149: \nL150: cite101†The Svalbard Office L151: E-mail: postmottak@nfd.dep.no\nL152: Phone: + 47 91 69 40 40 (Kl. 09.00-16.00)\nL153: Address: Postboks 69, 9171 Longyearbyen\nL154: Visitor address: Vei 227-2, 9170 Longyearbyen\nL155: \nL156: cite102†Go to the top L157: \nL158: cite103†Government.no L159: ## About this website\nL160: \nL161:   * cite104†News Alert L162:   * cite105†Right of use and copyright for photos and video material L163:   * cite106†Manage cookies L164: \nL165: Responsible for the ministry's webpages:\nL166: Responsible editor: cite107†Lisa Nordøen L167: tvi@nfd.dep.noWeb-editor: cite108†David G. Tvetene L168: hja@nfd.dep.noPhone: + 47 22 24 90 90\nL169: E-mail: postmottak@nfd.dep.no\nL170: Organisation number: 912 660 680\nL171: \nL172: \nL173: \nL174: ###\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn18view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn17search11\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNunavut - Canada.ca (https://www.canada.ca/en/canadian-heritage/services/provincial-territorial-symbols-canada/nunavut.html)\nciteturn18view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn17search0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 150\nL0:   * cite0†Skip to main content L1:   * cite1†Skip to \"About government\" L2:   * cite2†Skip to section menu L3: \nL4: ## Language selection\nL5: \nL6:   * cite3†fr (Français) L7: \nL8: cite4†/ Gouvernement du Canada L9: \nL10: ## Search\nL11: \nL12: Search Canada.ca [Input: Search Canada.ca]\nL13: \nL14: Search\nL15: \nL16: * * *\nL17: \nL18: ## Menu\nL19: \nL20: Main Menu\nL21: \nL22: ## You are here:\nL23: # Nunavut's territorial symbols\nL24: \nL25: The largest, northernmost and newest territory of Canada, Nunavut was officially separated from the Northwest Territories in 1999. The creation of Nunavut resulted in the first major change to the Canadian map since the incorporation of the province of Newfoundland and Labrador in 1949.\nL26: \nL27:   * cite5†Origin of the name L28:   * cite6†History L29:   * cite7†Coat of arms L30:   * cite8†Motto L31:   * cite9†Flag L32:   * cite10†Floral emblem L33:   * cite11†Other territorial symbols L34:     * cite12†Animal L35:     * cite13†Bird L36: ## Origin of the name\nL37: \nL38: Nunavut means “our land” in the Inuit language of Inuktitut.\nL39: \nL40: Population (2006): 30,800\nL41: \nL42: Total area: 1,994,000 km^{2} Capital: Iqaluit Date of creation: April 1, 1999 cite14†Image: The Nunavut territory highlighted within the map of Canada L43: ## History\nL44: \nL45: The earliest traces of settlement in Canada’s Arctic date back some 4,000 years, when the first of a number of hunting societies travelled across the Bering Strait into Northern Canada. The Inuit, who make up 85 percent of Nunavut’s population, are descended from one of these societies, the Thule, whose presence dates back at least 1,000 years.\nL46: The first European contact with the Inuit came with the arrival of the Norsemen in the 11th century. They were followed by explorers in search of the Northwest Passage — first in Elizabethan times and, again, in the 19th century. Later came whalers, Hudson’s Bay Company traders, missionaries and the Royal Canadian Mounted Police.\nL47: The idea of dividing the Northwest Territories in two was first raised during the government of John Diefenbaker in the early 1960s. The Government went as far as introducing a bill in the House of Commons to divide the Northwest Territories. The bill was allowed to die on the Order Paper after some residents of the Eastern Arctic flew to Ottawa and told a House of Commons Committee that the people of the North had not been consulted on the matter.\nL48: The Government appointed a three-man commission to examine political development in the Canadian Arctic and to make recommendations to Parliament. The Carrothers Commission held some hearings in the north and recommended that the issue of dividing the Northwest Territories be further examined in 10 years’ time.\nL49: In 1971, the leadership of the newly created Inuit Tapirisat of Canada stated its objective of creating a new territory for the Inuit in the Eastern Arctic. They insisted that it had to be part of any final land claims agreement, in spite of objections from both the Northwest Territories government in Yellowknife and the federal government. They were successful in persuading the Northwest Territories Legislative Assembly to put the issue to public debate.\nL50: The first major test for Nunavut came in the plebiscite of 1982 in which all residents of the Northwest Territories were asked to vote on the issue of division. Proponents of division won the plebiscite 53 percent to 47 percent. Ten years of negotiations on a boundary for the new territory were followed by another plebiscite in 1992 to ratify the boundary.\nL51: The Government of Canada then agreed to include the creation of Nunavut as part of the final land claims agreement that recognized Inuit ownership of 350,000 square kilometres of land. The Nunavut Act was passed by Parliament in 1993 and came into effect on April 1, 1999.\nL52: Nunavut accounts for roughly 20 percent of Canada’s land mass, making it the country’s largest political subdivision. The territory is governed by a 19-member legislative assembly that operates consensus‑style without political parties. Nunavut’s largest employer is the government. Other important economic sectors include mining, construction, tourism and traditional activities such as hunting, trapping, fishing, and arts and crafts.\nL53: \nL54: cite15†Image: Map of Nunavut L55: ## Coat of arms\nL56: \nL57: The Nunavut coat of arms was granted by the Right Honourable Roméo LeBlanc, Governor General of Canada, on April 1, 1999. The colours blue and gold symbolize the riches of the land, sea and sky. In the base of the shield, the inuksuk symbolizes the stone monuments that guide the people on the land and mark sacred and other special places. The qulliq, or Inuit stone lamp, represents the light and warmth of family and community.\nL58: Above, the concave arc of five gold circles refers to the life-giving properties of the sun, which arches above and below the horizon depending on the season. Also included is the North Star, Niqirtsituk, the traditional guide for navigation. More broadly, the star represents the unchanging nature of the leadership of elders in the community.\nL59: In the crest, the igloo represents the traditional life of the people and the means of survival. It also symbolizes the assembled members of the legislature meeting together for the good of Nunavut. The Royal Crown symbolizes government by and for all the people of Nunavut and the equivalent status of Nunavut with other territories and provinces in Canadian Confederation.\nL60: The tuktu (caribou) and qilalugaq tugaalik (narwhal) refer to land and sea animals that are part of the rich natural heritage of Nunavut and provide sustenance for its people. The compartment at the base is composed of land and sea and features three important species of Arctic wild flowers.\nL61: \nL62: cite16†Image: The Coat of Arms of Nunavut L63: ## Motto\nL64: \nL65: Nunavut sanginivut (Nunavut, our strength)\nL66: \nL67: ## Flag\nL68: \nL69: The flag of Nunavut was granted by the Right Honourable Roméo LeBlanc, Governor General of Canada, on April 1, 1999. It features the colours white, blue and gold, which symbolize the riches of the land, sea and sky. Red is a reference to Canada. The inuksuk and the Niqirtsituk star are also found on the coat of arms. The flag’s proportions are two by length and one by width.\nL70: \nL71: cite17†Image: The flag of Nunavut L72: ## Floral emblem\nL73: \nL74: Nunavut’s floral emblem, the purple saxifrage (Saxifraga oppositifolia), was unanimously adopted by the Legislative Assembly of Nunavut in 2000. This wildflower is one of the three flowers depicted on the coat of arms of Nunavut. One of the first plants to flower in the Arctic spring, the purple saxifrage often grows together across the tundra; it provides a vivid, purple contrast to the snow that is often present when they flower.\nL75: cite18†Image: The floral emblem of Nunavut, the purple saxifrage L76: ## Other territorial symbols\nL77: \nL78: ### Animal\nL79: \nL80: The Canadian Inuit dog (Canis familiaris borealis) is the official animal of Nunavut. Called “Qimmiq” in Inuktitut, this dog is one of the world’s oldest pure breeds and is known to have been resident in the Arctic for at least 4,000 years. It has been essential to the survival of the Inuit for generation, being the only working animal for long-range travel and a willing and capable hunting companion.\nL81: \nL82: cite19†Image: The animal of Nunavut, the Canadian Inuit dog L83: ### Bird\nL84: \nL85: While most other birds migrate south in the early fall, Nunavut’s official bird – the rock ptarmigan (Lagopus Mutus) – is truly an arctic bird, living in Nunavut throughout the year. Called “Aqiggiq” in Inuktitut, the rock ptarmigan has earned a place in the art, folklore and diet of indigenous peoples.\nL86: Distinct from most other birds with its three variations of plumage instead of the usual two, the rock ptarmigan faces its biggest challenge to survival in the winter, when snow and wind conditions make food and shelter hard to find. Features of the bird mirror human adaptations to the harsh Arctic surroundings, such as its feet feathered right down to the toes. This helps keep the rock ptarmigan warm, acting as a pair of feathery snowshoes to prevent the bird from sinking into the snow.\nL87: cite20†Image: The bird of Nunavut, the rock ptarmigan L88: ## Page details\nL89: \nL90: 2017-08-15\nL91: \nL92: ## Section Menu\nL93: \nL94: ### cite21†Provincial and territorial symbols L95: \nL96:   * cite22†About provincial and territorial symbols L97:   * cite23†Ontario L98:   * cite24†Quebec L99:   * cite25†Nova Scotia L100:   * cite26†New Brunswick L101:   * cite27†Manitoba L102:   * cite28†British Columbia L103:   * cite29†Prince Edward Island L104:   * cite30†Saskatchewan L105:   * cite31†Alberta L106:   * cite32†Newfoundland and Labrador L107:   * cite33†Northwest Territories L108:   * cite34†Yukon L109:   * cite35†Nunavut L110: \nL111: ## About this site\nL112: \nL113: ### Canadian Heritage\nL114: \nL115:   * cite36†Contact us L116: ### Government of Canada\nL117: \nL118:   * cite37†All contacts L119:   * cite38†Departments and agencies L120:   * cite39†About government L121: #### Themes and topics\nL122:   * cite40†Jobs L123:   * cite41†Immigration and citizenship L124:   * cite42†Travel and tourism†travel.gc.ca L125:   * cite43†Business L126:   * cite44†Benefits L127:   * cite45†Health L128:   * cite46†Taxes L129:   * cite47†Environment and natural resources L130:   * cite48†National security and defence L131:   * cite49†Culture, history and sport L132:   * cite50†Policing, justice and emergencies L133:   * cite51†Transport and infrastructure L134:   * cite52†Canada and the world†www.international.gc.ca L135:   * cite53†Money and finances L136:   * cite54†Science and innovation L137:   * cite55†Indigenous Peoples L138:   * cite56†Veterans and military L139:   * cite57†Youth L140:   * cite58†Manage life events L141: ### Government of Canada Corporate\nL142: \nL143:   * cite59†Social media L144:   * cite60†Mobile applications L145:   * cite61†About Canada.ca L146:   * cite6